# fleet.env.example — template for ~/.claude/fleet.env
#
#   cp claude-config/fleet.env.example ~/.claude/fleet.env    # then edit the paths
#
# WHY THIS TEMPLATE IS COMMITTED BUT THE REAL FILE IS NOT. The values name one product's
# checkout on one machine, which is exactly what dotfiles must not carry — a hardcoded
# `goals-onchain` here would silently point every other product's fleet at one product's lanes.
# But leaving it entirely uncommitted meant the file's *expected contents* existed nowhere
# except prose in three comment blocks, so a lost `fleet.env` or a second machine had nothing
# to reconstruct from. Found in review, answering "is any state now only in a machine-local
# file with no committed record?" — it was.
#
# WHO READS IT, and why it is a file rather than an export:
#   ~/.claude/scripts/_fleet.sh   → and therefore team-boot.sh, fleet-status.sh,
#                                   fleet-layout.sh, agent-fanout.sh, register-agent.sh
#   ~/.claude/hooks/lane-guard.sh → sources it directly, to stay dependency-free
#   ~/.zshrc                      → sources + exports, for interactive shells
#
# lane-guard.sh and register-agent.sh run as HOOKS, inside a claude process whose environment
# was frozen when that claude launched. An export added to .zshrc therefore reaches the next
# shell but NOT the fleet that is already running — and lane-guard's "this lane does not exist"
# branch exits 0, so a stale value turns the guard off for every agent and says nothing about
# it. A file is re-read at call time by whoever needs it, with no inheritance chain to break.
#
# Every entry defers to an existing environment variable, so a per-invocation export still wins.
# Keep that `${VAR:-...}` form when you add one.

# WHERE THE LANES LIVE — one directory holding `<agent-name>/` per lane, agent name == branch
# name == folder name, lane number == the trailing digits (`feature-3` → 3, `team-lead` → 0).
#
# THIS SHOULD BE THE MAIN CLONE'S OWN `.claude/worktrees/`, not a sibling directory. Every
# teammate boots in the lead's worktree and must `EnterWorktree` into its lane as its first
# act; that call is gated by a permission prompt no rule and no "don't ask again" suppresses,
# and the carve-out we rely on is exactly that path. Lanes anywhere else mean a human approves
# a prompt per agent on every restart.
#
# Unset, the fleet derives `<main clone>/.claude/worktrees` from the git common dir, which is
# correct for the common case — set it explicitly only when your lanes are somewhere else.
WORKFLOW_LANES_DIR="${WORKFLOW_LANES_DIR:-$HOME/git/YOUR-CLONE/.claude/worktrees}"
